[ti:The Power of the Press]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.17]Lesson 45 | 第45课
[00:02.26]The power of the press | 新闻媒体的威力
[00:09.99]Does the writer think the parents were lucky or unlucky to gain prosperity in this way? Why? | 作者认为这对父母以这种方式获得财富是幸运还是不幸？为什么？
[00:20.22]In democratic countries any efforts to restrict the freedom of the press are rightly condemned. | 在民主国家里，任何限制新闻自由的企图都会理所当然地受到谴责。
[00:28.59]However, this freedom can easily be abused. | 然而，这种自由很容易被滥用。
[00:33.74]Stories about people often attract far more public attention than political events. | 关于人的故事常常比政治事件更能吸引公众的注意。
[00:40.73]Though we may enjoy reading about the lives of others, | 虽然我们喜欢阅读有关别人生活的报道，
[00:44.94]it is extremely doubtful whether we would equally enjoy reading about ourselves. | 但我们是否同样喜欢读到关于自己的报道，就非常值得怀疑了。
[00:51.76]Acting on the contention that facts are sacred, | 以“事实是神圣的”这一论点为依据，
[00:56.16]reporters can cause untold suffering to individuals by publishing details about their private lives. | 记者们可以通过发表有关他人私生活的细节，给个人带来难以言表的痛苦。
[01:05.83]Newspapers exert such tremendous influence that they can not only bring about major changes to the lives of ordinary people but can even overthrow a government. | 报纸的影响如此巨大，不仅能够给普通人的生活带来重大变化，甚至能推翻一个政府。
[01:19.22]The story of a poor family that acquired fame and fortune overnight, dramatically illustrates the power of the press. | 一个一夜之间名利双收的穷苦家庭的故事，戏剧性地说明了新闻媒体的威力。
[01:28.30]The family lived in Aberdeen, a small town of 23, 000 inhabitants in South Dakota. | 这家人住在南达科他州一个人口仅为2.3万的小镇阿伯丁。
[01:36.02]As the parents had five children, life was a perpetual struggle against poverty. | 由于父母已有5个孩子，生活是一场与贫困的持久战。
[01:42.12]They were expecting their sixth child and were faced with even more pressing economic problems. | 他们即将迎来第6个孩子，面临更加紧迫的经济问题。
[01:49.51]If they had only had one more child, the fact would have passed unnoticed. | 如果他们只是再多生一个孩子，这件事本来会无人注意。
[01:55.53]They would have continued to struggle against economic odds and would have lived in obscurity. | 他们会继续在经济困境中挣扎，默默无闻地生活下去。
[02:02.51]But they suddenly became the parents of quintuplets, four girls and a boy, an event which radically changed their lives. | 但他们突然成了五胞胎——4个女孩和1个男孩——的父母，这件事彻底改变了他们的生活。
[02:12.99]The day after the birth of the five children, an aeroplane arrived in Aberdeen bringing sixty reporters and photographers. | 五胞胎出生的第二天，一架飞机飞抵阿伯丁，带来了60名记者和摄影师。
[02:22.41]The rise to fame was swift. | 成名之迅速可想而知。
[02:24.75]Television cameras and newspapers carried the news to everyone in the country. | 电视摄像机和报纸把消息传给了全国的每一个人。
[02:30.03]Newspapers and magazines offered the family huge sums for the exclusive rights to publish stories and photographs. | 报纸和杂志向这家人支付巨额款项，以获得独家发表故事和照片的权利。
[02:38.52]Gifts poured in not only from unknown people, | 礼物源源不断地涌来，不仅来自素不相识的人，
[02:42.22]but from baby food and soap manufacturers who wished to advertise their products. | 还来自希望为自己产品做广告的婴儿食品和肥皂制造商。
[02:48.83]The old farmhouse the family lived in was to be replaced by a new $500, 000 home. | 这家人居住的旧农舍将被一座价值50万美元的新住宅所取代。
[02:56.83]Reporters kept pressing for interviews so lawyers had to be employed to act as spokesmen for the family at press conferences. | 记者们不断强行要求采访，因此不得不雇请律师在新闻发布会上充当这家人的发言人。
[03:06.84]While the five babies were still quietly sleeping in oxygen tents in a hospital nursery, | 当五个婴儿还在医院育婴室里安静地睡在氧气帐中时，
[03:13.58]their parents were paying the price for fame. | 他们的父母却在为名声付出代价。
[03:17.27]It would never again be possible for them to lead normal lives. | 他们再也不能过正常的生活了。
[03:22.20]They had become the victims of commercialization, for their names had acquired a market value. | 他们已成为商业化的受害者，因为他们的名字有了市场价值。
[03:30.25]Instead of being five new family members, these children had immediately become a commodity. | 这些孩子没有成为五个新的家庭成员，而是立即变成了一种商品。
